‚¡Õç5j˙Owy≥vyj&x–m% dùZ 8Òg‘π{"rendered":{"html":"<p>Lists hold ordered values of one element type. GCSL's loops are\nexpressions, so a loop can collect results into a list or run only for its\neffects. This avoids a separate collection-building language.</p>\n<h2 id=\"language-lists-and-control-flow--lists-and-ranges\">Lists and ranges <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#language-lists-and-control-flow--lists-and-ranges\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><code>List&lt;Int&gt;</code> and <code>Int[]</code> name the same type. Indexes start at zero; a\nnegative or out-of-range index is a runtime error. A spread inserts\nanother list's elements. An empty list needs an expected element type:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let empty: List&lt;Int&gt; = []\nlet values = [...empty, 3, 8, 5]\nvalues[1]\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>0..3</code> produces <code>[0, 1, 2]</code>; <code>0..=3</code> includes <code>3</code>. Bounds are integers and\nranges run upward. A range used directly as a loop's iterable is counted\nwithout first allocating the list of all its integers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"language-lists-and-control-flow--collecting-and-repeating\">Collecting and repeating <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#language-lists-and-control-flow--collecting-and-repeating\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A loop used as a value collects each iteration's result. A guard skips\niterations that don't match:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let doubled = for n in [1, 2, 3] if n &gt; 1 { n * 2 }\ndoubled\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>A loop used as a statement discards its results. Use <code>for condition</code> for\nrepetition while a condition holds, <code>for { ... }</code> without a condition,\nor <code>do { ... } for condition</code> to test after the body. A header can contain\ninitialization and an update:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let values = for let i = 0; i &lt; 3; i := i + 1 { i * 2 }\nvalues\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>A plain header variable can be rebound by the header. Write <code>let mut</code> if\nthe body must also update it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"language-lists-and-control-flow--leaving-a-computation\">Leaving a computation <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#language-lists-and-control-flow--leaving-a-computation\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><code>break</code> ends the enclosing loop and <code>continue</code> skips to its next\niteration. Either can supply a value to the collecting loop before\nleaving that iteration. An unlabeled <code>return</code> returns from the enclosing\nfunction or <code>lazy</code> body.</p>\n<p>Labels identify an outer loop or a block. A labeled <code>return</code> leaves that\ntarget with one value; it does not append to a loop's accumulated list.\nControl flow cannot jump out through a function boundary. See <code>gcsl(5)</code>\nfor label syntax and the distinction between collected and returned\nvalues.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"language-lists-and-control-flow--lists-and-ranges","title":"Lists and ranges","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"language-lists-and-control-flow--collecting-and-repeating","title":"Collecting and repeating","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"language-lists-and-control-flow--leaving-a-computation","title":"Leaving a computation","number":null}],"has_mermaid":false,"dangling":[],"warnings":[],"broken":[]},"images":[],"links":{"pages":[],"anchors":[]},"refs":null}